.TH SUDO 1
.SH NAME
sudo \- run a command as root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo
.I command
[\ \fIargument\fR\ ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sudo
runs one command as root, for an account allowed to, after a password:
a step between
.BR su (1),
which gives a whole root shell, and no root at all.
.PP
.nf
	% sudo shutdown \-h now
	Password for root:
.fi
.PP
/etc/default/sudo says who may use it and whose password it asks for:
.TP 12
USERS=
the login names allowed, separated by commas or spaces, as
.BR "USERS=alex, pat" .
Root always may, and is not asked for a password.
.TP
PASSWORD=
.B root
(the default) asks for root's password, as
.B su
does;
.B self
asks for the account's own.
.PP
An account whose password is empty is not asked for it. The password is
not shown as it is typed; after three wrong ones,
.B sudo
gives up. Every use, allowed or not, is written at the end of
/usr/adm/sudolog, with the time, the account and the command.
.PP
The command is looked for along PATH, and runs with the same
environment.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
/etc/default/sudo
who may use sudo, and whose password
.TP
/usr/adm/sudolog
every use
.TP
/etc/passwd
the passwords
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"may not use sudo" for an account not in USERS; "3 wrong passwords";
"not found" for a command that cannot be run. The exit status is 1
after any of these, 2 after a mistake in the arguments, and the
command's own otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
su(1), passwd(1), crypt(3), getpass(3), shutdown(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B sudo
was first written in 1980 at the State University of New York at
Buffalo.
.SH AUTHORS
This sudo was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
It asks for the password every time; it does not remember it for a
few minutes, as some do.
.PP
It must be owned by root and set-user-id, as installed; copied
elsewhere without that, it cannot become root.
